gardless of their current status. Continuing through the rest of the planned experiments from both sources, I'm seeing exp-baseline-comparison, exp-multiscale-lzm, exp-post-rl, exp-extra-benchmarks, and exp-domain-analysis listed in CLAUDE.md, plus several more from the YAML file like exp-two-sided, exp-extended-sim, exp-empirical-null, exp-power-theory, and exp-leaderboard-data-recon. There's some inconsistency between what's marked as planned in the YAML versus what's marked DONE in CLAUDE.md, so I need to clarify which source to prioritize for the user's request. I need to look more carefully at the actual structure of the experiment_plan in CLAUDE.md since it doesn't seem to have explicit status fields like I initially thought—it's organized differently with id, claim, and description fields instead. Looking at the rest of the experiment registry, I can see there are quite a few more planned experiments beyond the initial batch—things like the IRT refit expansion, 4PL ablation studies, baseline comparisons, and several others focused on multiscale analysis, post-RL evaluation, and domain-specific work.\n\nThe issue is that the experiment_plan.yaml file seems out of sync with the actual registry. Many of these experiments are marked as \"planned\" in the CLAUDE.md documentation, but the registry itself shows them as already completed. This suggests the plan file hasn't been refreshed to reflect what's actually been done.\n\nLet me check the registry against what's listed as planned to identify which experiments are genuinely still in progress versus which ones have already finished. I need to reconsider what the user is actually asking for. They want me to take every experiment marked as `status: planned` directly from the experiment_plan section and generate worst-case imagined results for each one. Let me identify all the planned experiments from that list: the ctrl-contam pilot, the full ctrl-contam, leaderboard-audit, natural-experiment, v1-irt-refit-expanded